<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aryabhata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/476">476</a>–c. 550</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aryabhata was an Indian mathematician and astronomer of the 476–c. 550. His work, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhatiya">Aryabhatiya</a>, dealt with arithmetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> and astronomy, including an accurate approximation of pi and the idea that the earth rotates on its axis. He is cited as one of the scientific achievements associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aryabhata.jpg" alt="Aryabhata" width="293" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cpjha13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aryabhatta_of_Bihar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the Aryabhatiya Aryabhata says that he was 23 years old in the year 3600 of the Kali Yuga, which corresponds to 499 CE and implies a birth in 476 CE. He called himself a native of Kusumapura or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. His name is properly spelled Aryabhata, not Aryabhatta: every astronomical text spells it so, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmagupta">Brahmagupta</a>'s references to him by name in more than a hundred places. His disciple Bhaskara I calls him āśmakīya, one belonging to the Aśmaka country, and some have argued for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> origin.</p><p>He probably went to Kusumapura for advanced studies and lived there. A verse mentions him as head of an institution there, and it has been speculated that he headed the university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>, a link he never claimed himself. He is also reputed to have set up an observatory at the Sun temple in Taregana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He wrote several treatises, but only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhatiya">Aryabhatiya</a> survives. It has 108 verses and 13 introductory verses in four chapters, written in the terse style of sutra literature, so that commentators such as Bhaskara I and Nilakantha Somayaji (1465) explained its meaning. Its mathematics covers arithmetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, plane and spherical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a>, continued fractions, quadratic equations and a table of sines. The Arya-siddhanta, now lost, is known through Varahamihira, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmagupta">Brahmagupta</a> and Bhaskara I.</p><p>He gave an approximation of π, 62,832 for a circle of diameter 20,000, accurate to two parts in a million. His kuṭṭaka method solved first-order indeterminate equations. In astronomy he stated that the Earth is round and rotates daily on its axis, so that the stars' apparent westward motion is relative, comparing it to a man in a boat who sees the shore moving backward. His model of the Solar System was geocentric, with epicycles. He explained eclipses by shadows, not by Rahu and Ketu, and calculated the sidereal rotation as 23 hours, 56 minutes and 4.1 seconds.</p><p>His work shaped Indian astronomy and, through an Arabic translation of about 820 CE, influenced the Islamic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">Al-Khwarizmi</a> cites it, and the term sine descends from his jya. His calendar calculations are still used for the Panchangam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aryabhata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Aryabhata</b>, whose Aryabhatiya dates to 499 CE, approximated pi to four decimal places and proposed that the earth rotates on its axis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aryabhata</b>, in 499, and Brahmagupta, in 628, wrote works of astronomy and mathematics in Sanskrit verse, Brahmagupta setting out rules for calculating with zero and negative numbers; the medical treatises of Charaka and Sushruta founded Ayurveda; and the schools of Hindu, Buddhist and Jain philosophy, from Nagarjuna to…</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Indian tradition holds other dates: <b>Aryabhata</b>'s 3102 BCE has become widespread, the Aihole inscription of Pulakeshin II implies 3137 BCE, and Varāhamihira and Kalhana place the war around 2449 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=524" aria-label="Pataliputra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aryabhata"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4626 5367h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4626 5367h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.61,85.14&amp;year=524">What was here in 524 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
